-- title:
--   Diagonal families covering hyperbolic classes realise and separate ratios
-- statement:
--   Let $K$ be a number field, and let $\Delta_K$ be a set of elements of $\mathrm{GL}_2(K)$. The hypothesis `hgenK` asks that every $K$-algebra automorphism of $K$ lie in the subgroup generated by the identity, which is what is needed to form the twisted-norm class map for $\sigma = 1$. Three assumptions are made on $\Delta_K$: (i) each $t\in\Delta_K$ is diagonal, i.e. its matrix entries in positions $(1,0)$ and $(0,1)$ vanish, and $\mathrm{Algebra.norm}_K$ of the ratio $t_{00}/t_{11}$ is not $1$ (the norm for the extension $K/K$, so this says the ratio itself is not $1$); (ii) for distinct $t,t'\in\Delta_K$ the sets $\{\delta : \exists g,\ t^{-1}\,g^{-1}\delta\, g \in Z(\mathrm{GL}_2(K))\}$ and the same set for $t'$ are disjoint, where the twisting by the identity ring homomorphism of $K$ is trivial, so these are the sets of $\delta$ conjugate to $t$, respectively $t'$, modulo the centre; (iii) every $\delta$ whose class under the $\sigma$-conjugacy relation $\delta_1\sim\delta_2 \iff \exists k,\ \delta_2 = k^{-1}\delta_1 k^{\sigma}$ (with $\sigma = 1$) is sent by [`LT.TwistedNorm.normClassMap`](def/TwistedNormClasses.html#L766) to the conjugacy class of some $\gamma$ whose characteristic polynomial factors as $(X-a)(X-b)$ with $a\neq b$ lies in the union over $t\in\Delta_K$ of the sets in (ii). The conclusion is twofold: first, for every unit $u$ of $K$ with $u\neq 1$ there is $t\in\Delta_K$ with $t_{00}/t_{11}=u$ or $t_{00}/t_{11}=u^{-1}$; second, for distinct $t,t'\in\Delta_K$ the ratio $t_{00}/t_{11}$ is different from both $t'_{00}/t'_{11}$ and its inverse.
--
--   This is the bookkeeping step which says that a family of diagonal elements whose conjugacy classes modulo the centre are pairwise disjoint and cover the regular split (hyperbolic) twisted-norm classes is indexed, bijectively up to inversion, by the eigenvalue ratios: every ratio $u\neq 1$ occurs and no ratio occurs twice, even up to inversion. It is used in the comparison of hyperbolic contributions, where the sum over such a family of diagonal representatives is rewritten as a sum over ratios.
-- source:
